WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 218: Germany: Martin Luther, the 95 Theses, and the Protestant Reformation

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.600
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.720
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:11.440
[Rebecca] Today we cross into the forested heart of Europe to explore the history of Germany.

4
00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:19.1000
[Rebecca] In the 16th century, a solitary German monk armed with a quill and printing press challenged the religious and imperial order of Europe.

5
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:26.160
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of Martin Luther, the 95 Theses and the Diet of Worms.

6
00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:40.880
[George] Born in November 1483 in the mining town of Isleben in Saxony, Martin Luther was the son of a stern, ambitious copper leaseholder who sent him to the University of Erfurt to study law.

7
00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:49.920
[George] But in July 1505, while riding on the road near Stotternheim, 21-year-old Luther was caught in a terrifying summer thunderstorm.

8
00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:55.040
[George] A jagged bolt of lightning struck the ground beside him, throwing him from his horse.

9
00:00:55.040 --> 00:01:06.240
[George] Gripped by paralyzing terror of sudden death and the unyielding judgment of God, Luther cried out to the patron saint of miners, Help me, Saint Anne, I will become a monk.

10
00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:15.520
[George] Spurning his father's fury and abandoning his legal studies, Luther entered the strict, cloistered monastery of the Augustinian hermits in Erfurt.

11
00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:23.520
[George] Plagued by an agonizing spiritual crisis known as Anfitchtung, an overwhelming dread of divine wrath and personal unworthiness.

12
00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:36.480
[George] Luther confessed his sins for hours every day, fasted until he fainted, and slept on bare stone in winter, but found no peace in medieval monastic rituals, crying out in tormented despair.

13
00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:39.040
[George] I hated him.

14
00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:43.680
[George] Trapped on an open road outside Erfurt during a terrifying July tempest.

15
00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:54.000
[George] Desperately bargaining with heaven, the terrified law student cried out to St.

16
00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:03.160
[George] Anne, the patroness of miners Save me, and I will become a monk, setting in motion a religious revolution that transformed the Western world.

17
00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:09.880
[George] In 1510, Luther was dispatched on monastic business to Rome, the holy capital of Christendom.

18
00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:15.800
[George] Walking barefoot across the Alps, Luther expected to find an earthly paradise of holiness.

19
00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:24.360
[George] Instead, he was shocked and disillusioned by the worldly corruption, cynicism, and moral decay of the papal court of Pope Julius I.

20
00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:33.000
[George] Roman priests mocked the sacred mass, speeding through rituals in minutes to collect fees, and opulent brothels catered to the clergy.

21
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:43.160
[George] Climbing the sacred scala sancto on his knees while reciting the Lord's Prayer to free his grandfather's soul from purgatory, Luther stood up at the top and doubted aloud.

22
00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:45.000
[George] Who knows if it is so?

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:49.160
[George] Transferred to the provincial frontier town of Wittenberg to teach theology.

24
00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:52.120
[George] Luther studied the New Testament Greek text.

25
00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:59.000
[George] In the tower room of the monastery, reading the Apostle Paul's epistle to the Romans, light broke through his soul.

26
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:01.080
[George] The just shall live by faith.

27
00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:05.960
[George] Luther discovered the revolutionary doctrine of justification by faith alone.

28
00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:15.160
[George] Salvation was not a commercial transaction earned by human merits or penances, but an unearned gift of divine grace received through faith alone.

29
00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:31.720
[George] When Luther travelled to Rome in 1510 on monastic business, he expected to find a holy city of apostolic purity, but instead encountered worldly renaissance cardinals, ostentatious corruption, and priests who rushed through masses with blasphemous cynicism.

30
00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:46.000
[George] Returning to Wittenberg, Luther immersed himself in the Greek text of the New Testament, where his deep study of the epistle to the Romans unlocked the liberating conviction that human salvation is an unmerited gift of divine grace through faith alone.

31
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:54.960
[George] In 1517, the quiet university town of Wittenberg was roiled by the most shameless commercial fundraising campaign in church history.

32
00:03:54.960 --> 00:04:00.240
[George] Pope Leo X, needing millions to finance the construction of the monumental Saint.

33
00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:13.040
[George] Peter's Basilica in Rome, and the young aristocratic Prince Albert of Mainz, needing cash to pay back the Fugger banking dynasty for buying three archbishoprics, launched a massive sale of plenary indulgences.

34
00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:24.400
[George] They dispatched the flamboyant Dominican friar Johann Tetzel through Germany, riding into towns with papal bulls carried on velvet cushions, accompanied by trumpets and drums.

35
00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:34.320
[George] Tetzel set up a red cross in the marketplace and terrorized the populace with graphic descriptions of their dead parents weeping in purgatory, singing his kegjijingle.

36
00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:51.280
[George] As soon as the coin in the coffer rings, the soul from purgatory springs, parishioners flocked across the river from Wittenberg to purchase letters promising instant pardon for sins past, present, and future, returning home to boast they no longer needed repentance or confession.

37
00:04:51.280 --> 00:05:03.040
[George] In 1517, the Dominican friar Johann Tetzel crossed into the territories bordering Saxony, pitching papal indulgence certificates like a traveling auctioneer to fund the construction of St.

38
00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:05.120
[George] Peter's Basilica in Rome.

39
00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:14.320
[George] Tetzel shamelessly promised that even the most heinous sins would be forgiven instantly and purgatorial torments erased, chanting his notorious rhyme.

40
00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:18.960
[George] As soon as the coin in the coffer rings, the soul from purgatory springs.

41
00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:27.200
[George] Outraged by the predatory exploitation of the poor and the destruction of true spiritual repentance, Father Martin Luther acted.

42
00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:44.280
[George] On All Hallows Eve, October 31, 1517, the day before thousands of pilgrims gathered at the Castle Church of All Saints in Wittenberg to venerate Elector Frederick the Wise's massive collection of 19,000 holy relics, Luther walked to the church doors.

43
00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:54.840
[George] Using a heavy hammer, he nailed a placard containing 95 academic debating propositions written in Latin, the disputation on the power and efficacy of indulgences.

44
00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:59.400
[George] Luther did not intend to split the church, he sought academic debate.

45
00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:09.000
[George] Thesis 27 argued, they preach only human doctrines who say that as soon as the coin clinks into the money chest, the soul flies out of purgatory.

46
00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:15.720
[George] Thesis 82 asked with biting logic, why does not the Pope empty purgatory for the sake of holy love?

47
00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:19.560
[George] Rather than for miserable money with which to build a basilica.

48
00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:32.440
[George] Translated into German by students and reproduced on the newly invented Gutenberg printing press, the 95 Theses spread across Germany in two weeks and across Europe in four weeks like a wild prairie fire.

49
00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:38.040
[George] On October 31, 1517, the eve of All Saints' Day, Dr.

50
00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:46.200
[George] Martin Luther strode up to the heavy wooden doors of All Saints' Church in Wittenberg and hammered up his 95 theses for academic debate.

51
00:06:46.200 --> 00:07:00.440
[George] Written in Latin, the theses sharply attacked the commercialization of divine mercy, ignited popular outrage across Germany when translated into the vernacular, and struck a devastating blow against papal financial dominance.

52
00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:09.720
[George] Over the next four years, as papal interrogators like Cardinal Cajetin and theologian Johann Eck tried to silence him, Luther's theology deepened.

53
00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:17.680
[George] He rejected the infallibility of popes and church councils, asserting the supreme authority of sola scriptura, scripture alone.

54
00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:28.720
[George] In June 1520, Pope Leo X issued the Papal Bull ex surge domine, condemning Luther as a wild boar invading the Lord's vineyard and threatening excommunication.

55
00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:36.800
[George] In December, before a crowd of cheering students outside Wittenberg's Elster Gate, Luther burned the papal bull in a bonfire.

56
00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:51.040
[George] In April 1521, summoned under royal safe conduct before the Imperial Assembly at the Diet of Worms, Luther stood before the 19-year-old Holy Roman Emperor Charles V, German princes and papal legates.

57
00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:57.040
[George] Gesturing to a table stacked with his books, the imperial prosecutor asked if he would recant.

58
00:07:57.040 --> 00:08:02.480
[George] After a night of agonizing prayer, Luther delivered his immortal, historic stand.

59
00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:10.720
[George] Unless I am convinced by the testimony of the scriptures or by clear reason, for I do not trust either in the Pope or in councils alone.

60
00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:20.320
[George] Since it is well known that they have often erred and contradicted themselves, I am bound by the scriptures I have quoted, and my conscience is captive to the Word of God.

61
00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:26.400
[George] I cannot and I will not recant anything, since it is neither safe nor right to go against conscience.

62
00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:29.360
[George] Here I stand, I can do no other.

63
00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:30.720
[George] God help me.

64
00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:31.680
[George] Amen.

65
00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:44.400
[George] Summoned before the Imperial Assembly at the Diet of Worms in April 1521, Luther stood alone before the resplendent court of Holy Roman Emperor Charles V, surrounded by papal legates and German princes.

66
00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:56.880
[George] Commanded to recant his books, Luther famously declared that his conscience was captive to the Word of God, concluding, unless I am convinced by Scripture and plain reason, I cannot and will not recant.

67
00:08:56.880 --> 00:08:59.440
[George] Here I stand, I can do no other.

68
00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:01.000
[George] God help me.

69
00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:01.880
[George] Amen.

70
00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:03.240
[George] Emperor Charles V.

71
00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:10.200
[George] declared Luther an outlaw and heretic under the Edict of Worms, mandating that anyone could kill him without penalty.

72
00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:17.480
[George] As Luther rode home along the edge of the Thuringian forest, masked horsemen with drawn swords ambushed his carriage.

73
00:09:17.480 --> 00:09:25.400
[George] But this was no murderous assassination, it was a brilliant rescue staged by Luther's protector, Prince Frederick the Wise.

74
00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:32.440
[George] Whisking Luther up to the isolated, mist-covered mountain fortress of the Wartburg, Frederick hid him in plain sight.

75
00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:54.040
[George] For ten months, Luther grew a thick black beard, wore noble robes, and adopted the disguise of junker Jurg, Knight George, while enduring bouts of depression, digestive agony, and legendary spiritual combats with the devil, whom he famously claimed to fight with prayer and ink, Luther achieved an astonishing literary miracle.

76
00:09:54.040 --> 00:10:02.040
[George] In just eleven weeks, using Erasmus's Greek text, Luther translated the entire New Testament into vernacular German.

77
00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:05.160
[George] Luther did not use stuffy court Latinisms.

78
00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:18.680
[George] He listened to mothers in their homes, children in the streets, and merchants in the market, forging a vibrant, rhythmic, idiomatic German prose that created the standard high German language and unified fragmented German culture.

79
00:10:18.680 --> 00:10:30.440
[George] Following the imperial ban declaring him an outlaw who could be murdered with impunity, Luther was secretly intercepted by friendly Saxon horsemen and hidden in the remote fortress of Wartburg Castle.

80
00:10:30.440 --> 00:10:44.840
[George] Disguised as Knight Georgia with a flowing beard and sword, Luther spent ten furious months translating the New Testament from original Greek into vigorous, expressive German, establishing the foundation of modern German literature.

81
00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:53.920
[George] Returning to Wittenberg to halt radical iconoclasts who were smashing church statues and stained glass, Luther instituted orderly reforms.

82
00:10:53.920 --> 00:11:05.680
[George] In 1525, shocking Catholic Europe, the former monk married Catharina von Bora, an escaped nun whom he had helped smuggle out of a convent hidden inside empty herring barrels.

83
00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:08.800
[George] Their marriage was a warm, boisterous partnership.

84
00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:19.520
[George] Katie managed the former monastery, brewed beer, raised six children, and engaged in deep theological banter recorded by students in Luther's Table Talk.

85
00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:42.240
[George] Luther composed beloved hymns, including A Mighty Fortress is Our God, giving the congregation a singing voice in worship, though Luther's legacy was marred in his bitter, aging years by vitriolic polemics against the peasants' war and hostile writings against the Jews, his fundamental breakthrough, the freedom of Christian conscience, the universal priesthood of all believers.

86
00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:54.160
[George] And the democratization of Scripture shattered the medieval feudal order, fostering universal literacy, public education, and the rise of democratic self-governance in the Western world.

87
00:11:54.160 --> 00:12:10.320
[George] In 1525, Luther scandalized Catholic Europe by marrying Catharina von Bora, an escaped Cistercian nun, creating an affectionate, bustling household in the former Wittenberg monastery that became the cultural template for the Protestant family.

88
00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:23.120
[George] Through his hymns, catechisms, and defiant theological writings, Martin Luther democratized literacy, championed the individual conscience, and fundamentally reshaped the course of world history.

89
00:12:23.760 --> 00:12:31.400
[Rebecca] Martin Luther's Stand for Conscience shattered the Holy Roman Empire into hundreds of independent German principalities.

90
00:12:31.640 --> 00:12:39.240
[Rebecca] Three centuries later, that fractured German world was forged into an iron empire by an indomitable statesman.

91
00:12:39.240 --> 00:12:57.960
[Rebecca] Next week, we continue our German saga with episode 219, Otto von Bismarck, Blood and Iron, and the Unification of Germany, the Master of Realpolitik, the Franco Prussian War, the Coronation of the Kaiser in the Hall of Mirrors at Versailles, and the Delicate Web of European Alliances.

92
00:12:57.960 --> 00:13:03.560
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

